-- =====================================================================
-- Session 496 migration — 2026-11-09 — canon / Matthew 20–24 — Phase 2 REBUILD, batch b4: the orphan cards rewritten Come-and-See and threaded.
-- 368 cards rewritten in place (ids and verse pairs kept), 11 new thread(s), 1 unanchored thread(s) anchored, 368 thread members added.
-- Drafted on: Claude Sonnet 5.5; read and corrected on Claude Fable 5.1; every note composed by script (assemble496.py): target verse whole
-- from parsed/canon.json (restored-names KJV, the canon convention), Matthew verse whole from the live text of the Nov 9 read-back, then the prose.
-- Safety (DO block, before any write): RAISES if a card id is gone or sits on another verse pair, if a Matthew verse's live text differs from the
-- read-back, if a card's live note is neither the note this was written against nor the new one, if a card is already in a thread this file does
-- not put it in, if a thread to join / an anchor verse is missing, or if a thread to anchor already has a different anchor.
-- Idempotent: a re-run reports 0 rewritten / 0 inserted / 0 anchored.  Nothing is deleted.
-- Run: python3 api/apply_migration.py data-schema/migrations/session496_matthew_b4_rebuild.sql
-- =====================================================================
\echo 'session496 — Matthew 20–24 rebuild starting...'
BEGIN;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b4_c (id BIGINT, src_ch INT, src_v INT, src_text TEXT, tgt_slug TEXT, tgt_ch INT, tgt_v INT, old_note TEXT, new_note TEXT) ON COMMIT DROP;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b4_t (slug TEXT, title TEXT, summary_md TEXT, ch INT, a_vn INT, z_vn INT, tier TEXT, sort_order INT) ON COMMIT DROP;

*Behold, we go up to Jerusalem; and the Son of Adam shall be betrayed unto the chief priests and unto the scribes, and they shall condemn him to death* (Matthew 20:18)

*And shall deliver him to the Gentiles to mock, and to scourge, and to crucify him: and the third day he shall rise again.* (Matthew 20:19)

The first telling named the elders, the chief priests and the scribes, and the city.

*From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* (Matthew 16:21)

The second came in Galilee.

*And while they abode in Galilee, Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, The Son of Adam shall be betrayed into the hands of men* (Matthew 17:22)

By the third telling he has said where, by whom and how, and the prophets had said the rest before him. Isaiah asked who would believe it.

*Who hath believed our report? and to whom is the arm of Yahuah (LORD) revealed?* (Isaiah 53:1)

Zechariah heard the sword called against the shepherd, and the sheep scattered after him.

*smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* (Zechariah 13:7)

The same prophet heard what the people of that city would one day do.

*they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son* (Zechariah 12:10)

Put together, the three tellings are not a surprise sprung on the twelve. They are the road the prophets had already walked out in words, and the twelve, who are told it privately, are the first to find it hard to hear.$n$,20,17,19,'free',368),
('matthew-21-faith-and-the-mountain-removed',$n$Faith that does not doubt, and the mountain cast into the sea$n$,$n$After the fig tree withers the disciples marvel, and he turns the wonder into a saying about faith.

*Verily I say unto you, If ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree, but also if ye shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; it shall be done.* (Matthew 21:21)

He had said the same about a mountain before, when the disciples could not cast the devil out of a child and he named their unbelief.

*If ye have faith as a grain of mustard seed, ye shall say unto this mountain, Remove hence to yonder place; and it shall remove; and nothing shall be impossible unto you.* (Matthew 17:20)

The mountains were already a way of speaking in the psalms and the prophets, and they were never the steady thing. The psalmist says the mountains may go into the sea and he will not fear, because Elohim (God) is his refuge.

*Therefore will not we fear, though the earth be removed, and though the mountains be carried into the midst of the sea;* (Psalm 46:2)

Isaiah says it from the other side. Mountains go, and what Yahuah (the LORD) has covenanted does not.

*For the mountains shall depart, and the hills be removed; but my kindness shall not depart from thee, neither shall the covenant of my peace be removed, saith Yahuah (LORD) that hath mercy on thee.* (Isaiah 54:10)

So faith that tells a mountain to be removed is leaning on the one who does not move. John gives the same promise a purpose, and a line about the commandments follows close behind it.

*And whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, that will I do, that the Father may be glorified in the Son.* (John 14:13)

*If ye love me, keep my commandments.* (John 14:15)

And when a man made whole is found in the temple, the healing is followed by a charge.

*Behold, thou art made whole: sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee.* (John 5:14)

Faith, asking, and what he does with the asking run in one line. The power is the Father's, the asking is in the Son's name, and what is given is given to someone who is then to walk straight.$n$,21,21,22,'free',369),
('matthew-21-by-what-authority',$n$By what authority: John's baptism and the voice from the cloud$n$,$n$The chief priests and the elders come to him in the temple while he is teaching and put the question straight.

*By what authority doest thou these things? and who gave thee this authority?* (Matthew 21:23)

He does not dodge it. He asks them one in return, and the whole answer is folded into it.

*The baptism of John, whence was it? from heaven, or of men?* (Matthew 21:25)

They cannot say heaven, because they would be asked why they did not believe him, and they will not say men, because they fear the people, who all hold John as a prophet. So the question about his authority is settled by what John was. Matthew had already told who John was, and Yahusha (Jesus) said he was more than a prophet.

*For this is he that was spoken of by the prophet Isaiah, saying, The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of Yahuah (Lord), make his paths straight.* (Matthew 3:3)

Then there is the voice from the cloud, where the Father names his Son and tells the disciples to hear him.

*While he yet spake, behold, a bright cloud overshadowed them: and behold a voice out of the cloud, which said, This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased; hear ye him.* (Matthew 17:5)

None of this was new when it was said. Moses had told the people how a prophet would be known, and where he would come from.

*Yahuah Elohayka (The LORD thy God) will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me; unto him ye shall hearken;* (Deuteronomy 18:15)

And he gave the credential itself, that Yahuah (the LORD) puts his words in the prophet's mouth.

*I will raise them up a Prophet from among their brethren, like unto thee, and will put my words in his mouth; and he shall speak unto them all that I shall command him.* (Deuteronomy 18:18)

So the credential is not a title from the temple. It is John's baptism, which they would not own, and the Father's voice, and the Prophet Moses said would come from among his brethren. The question was asked by men who held the offices of the house, and the answer was written in the book they read.$n$,21,23,27,'free',370),
('matthew-22-their-city-burned-and-the-house-left-desolate',$n$Their city burned and the house left desolate$n$,$n$It ain't new. A king sends servants with an invitation, the bidden seize them and kill them, and the king answers by burning their city. Yahusha (Jesus) tells it in the temple, and every piece of it was laid down before him.

*And the remnant took his servants, and entreated them spitefully, and slew them.* (Matthew 22:6)

*But when the king heard thereof, he was wroth: and he sent forth his armies, and destroyed those murderers, and burned up their city.* (Matthew 22:7)

The ones who slew the servants are the rulers who killed the prophets, and he says so outright in the next chapter.

*O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, thou that killest the prophets, and stonest them which are sent unto thee, how often would I have gathered thy children together, even as a hen gathereth her chickens under her wings, and ye would not!* (Matthew 23:37)

*Behold, your house is left unto you desolate.* (Matthew 23:38)

Jeremiah stood at the gate of the house in his own day and told the people what they were leaning on.

*Trust ye not in lying words, saying, The temple of Yahuah (LORD), The temple of Yahuah (LORD), The temple of Yahuah (LORD), are these.* (Jeremiah 7:4)

Isaiah had shown that when Yahuah (the LORD) sends an army against a people, the army is his rod and not his equal.

*O Assyrian, the rod of mine anger, and the staff in their hand is mine indignation.* (Isaiah 10:5)

Two chapters on, the disciples show him the buildings of the temple and he tells them what is coming.

*There shall not be left here one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down.* (Matthew 24:2)

What is judged in all of these is the house that was trusted for its own sake and the rulers who killed the ones sent to them. In the same sentence that leaves the house empty he speaks of gathering the children.$n$,22,6,7,'free',371),
('matthew-23-the-seat-of-moses-and-what-was-laid-on-it',$n$The seat of Moses and what was laid on it$n$,$n$It ain't new. The seat the scribes and the Pharisees sit in was set up by Moses, and Yahusha (Jesus) opens the chapter by telling the crowd to honor what is read from it and to leave the works alone:

*The scribes and the Pharisees sit in Moses’ seat* (Matthew 23:2)

*All therefore whatsoever they bid you observe, that observe and do; but do not ye after their works: for they say, and do not.* (Matthew 23:3)

The seat is in the Torah. A case too hard for the local court goes up to the priests and the judge, and what they teach out of the law is what is done:

*According to the sentence of the law which they shall teach thee, and according to the judgment which they shall tell thee, thou shalt do* (Deuteronomy 17:11)

So the law read from the seat stands. The fault is in the works and in the burdens laid on top of it, loads bound on other men's shoulders by hands that would not touch them. The same goes for the signs that were commanded in the first place. The fringe, the frontlet and the title of Father were all spoken of in Moses and the prophets before the Pharisees ever sat down, so the woe is not against any of them. It is against making them larger to be seen:

*But all their works they do for to be seen of men: they make broad their phylacteries, and enlarge the borders of their garments* (Matthew 23:5)

*Speak unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel), and bid them that they make them fringes in the borders of their garments throughout their generations* (Numbers 15:38)

Yahusha (Jesus) wore the border himself, and people reached for the hem of his garment to be healed. On the title, he sends the crowd back to the Father the prophets already named:

*And call no man your father upon the earth: for one is your Father, which is in heaven.* (Matthew 23:9)

*Doubtless thou art our father, though Abraham be ignorant of us* (Isaiah 63:16)$n$,23,2,9,'free',372),
('matthew-24-the-temple-torn-down-and-the-question',$n$The temple torn down, and the disciples' question on the mount of Olives$n$,$n$It ain't new. A house that bears the name of Yahuah (the LORD) has been told before that it can be taken down, and the line Yahusha (Jesus) speaks as he leaves the temple is the same one the prophets spoke at its gate: *There shall not be left here one stone upon another, that shall not be thrown down.* (Matthew 24:2)

Jeremiah gave Shiloh as the pattern: *Then will I make this house like Shiloh, and will make this city a curse to all the nations of the earth.* (Jeremiah 26:6) Micah says it of the hill itself: *Therefore shall Zion for your sake be plowed as a field, and Jerusalem shall become heaps, and the mountain of the house as the high places of the forest.* (Micah 3:12) In both places the fall is tied to what the people did, and Yahusha (Jesus) has just said the same: *Behold, your house is left unto you desolate.* (Matthew 23:38)

He does not say it dry-eyed. *And when he was come near, he beheld the city, and wept over it,* (Luke 19:41) and then he goes out and sits on the mount of Olives, the hill Zechariah named long before. There the disciples come to him alone with the question that the rest of the chapter answers: *Tell us, when shall these things be? and what shall be the sign of thy coming, and of the end of the world?* (Matthew 24:3) Mark and Luke carry the same question, so all three Gospels open the talk with a house falling and a hill where he sits.$n$,24,1,3,'free',373),
('matthew-24-the-beginning-of-sorrows',$n$The beginning of sorrows: deception, wars, hatred, and the witness to all nations$n$,$n$It ain't new. The first sorrows are the old ones, and the first word is a warning: *Take heed that no man deceive you.* (Matthew 24:4) Isaiah had already heard Yahuah (the LORD) say of Egypt, *And I will set the Egyptians against the Egyptians: and they shall fight every one against his brother, and every one against his neighbour; city against city, and kingdom against kingdom.* (Isaiah 19:2) Yahusha (Jesus) says it of the whole age: *For nation shall rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom: and there shall be famines, and pestilences, and earthquakes, in divers places.* (Matthew 24:7) He names them a start, not a finish: *All these are the beginning of sorrows.* (Matthew 24:8)

Hatred comes with them, and so does a call to stand: *And ye shall be hated of all men for my name’s sake: but he that endureth to the end shall be saved.* (Matthew 10:22) Inside the house the cost is quieter: *And because iniquity shall abound, the love of many shall wax cold.* (Matthew 24:12) Iniquity is lawlessness, the Torah set aside, and love going cold is what it does to a people.

Paul writes the same watch to the assembly: *Let no man deceive you by any means: for that day shall not come, except there come a falling away first, and that man of sin be revealed, the son of perdition;* (2 Thessalonians 2:3) And the sorrows carry a witness with them: *And this gospel of the kingdom shall be preached in all the world for a witness unto all nations; and then shall the end come.* (Matthew 24:14) Isaiah says where that witness is heading: *it shall come, that I will gather all nations and tongues; and they shall come, and see my glory.* (Isaiah 66:18)$n$,24,4,14,'free',374),
('matthew-24-the-abomination-of-desolation',$n$The abomination of desolation and the great tribulation$n$,$n$It ain't new. Yahusha (Jesus) sends the reader back to a named book: *When ye therefore shall see the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet, stand in the holy place, (whoso readeth, let him understand:)* (Matthew 24:15) The book itself says: *And he shall confirm the covenant with many for one week: and in the midst of the week he shall cause the sacrifice and the oblation to cease, and for the overspreading of abominations he shall make it desolate, even until the consummation, and that determined shall be poured upon the desolate.* (Daniel 9:27) The distress he names is Daniel's too: *And at that time shall Michael stand up, the great prince which standeth for the children of thy people: and there shall be a time of trouble, such as never was since there was a nation even to that same time: and at that time thy people shall be delivered, every one that shall be found written in the book.* (Daniel 12:1) Matthew says it in his own words: *For then shall be great tribulation, such as was not since the beginning of the world to this time, no, nor ever shall be.* (Matthew 24:21)

Paul keeps the same watch in his own letter and stays in it: *Let no man deceive you by any means: for that day shall not come, except there come a falling away first, and that man of sin be revealed, the son of perdition;* (2 Thessalonians 2:3)

In Revelation John is given a reed and told, *Rise, and measure the temple of Elohim (God), and the altar, and them that worship therein.* (Revelation 11:1) And of the beast, *there was given unto him a mouth speaking great things and blasphemies; and power was given unto him to continue forty and two months.* (Revelation 13:5)$n$,24,15,22,'free',375),
('matthew-24-false-christs-and-the-lightning',$n$False Christs and false prophets, the lightning, and the eagles$n$,$n$It ain't new. A sign or a wonder is no proof, and Moses said so first: *If there arise among you a prophet, or a dreamer of dreams, and giveth thee a sign or a wonder,* (Deuteronomy 13:1) and the verses that follow ask whether he turns you from the commandments. Yahusha (Jesus) says it again: *For there shall arise false Christs, and false prophets, and shall shew great signs and wonders; insomuch that, if it were possible, they shall deceive the very elect.* (Matthew 24:24) He tells them how not to be led: *And they shall say to you, See here; or, see there: go not after them, nor follow them.* (Luke 17:23)

His own coming needs no pointing out: *For as the lightning cometh out of the east, and shineth even unto the west; so shall also the coming of the Son of Adam be.* (Matthew 24:27)

The eagles follow it: *For wheresoever the carcase is, there will the eagles be gathered together.* (Matthew 24:28) Job's picture is plain, *Her young ones also suck up blood: and where the slain are, there is she.* (Job 39:30) and Hosea puts the eagle on the house that broke covenant: *Set the trumpet to thy mouth. He shall come as an eagle against the house of Yahuah (LORD), because they have transgressed my covenant, and trespassed against my law.* (Hosea 8:1) Revelation has a supper of its own: *Come and gather yourselves together unto the supper of the great Elohim (God);* (Revelation 19:17)$n$,24,23,28,'free',376),
('matthew-24-heaven-and-earth-pass-but-my-words-shall-not',$n$Heaven and earth pass, but his words do not$n$,$n$It ain't new. The fig tree gives the season: *Now learn a parable of the fig tree; When his branch is yet tender, and putteth forth leaves, ye know that summer is nigh:* (Matthew 24:32) The verse that closes the thought gives the measure: *Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away.* (Matthew 24:35)

He used the same measure on the mountain where he taught: *For verily I say unto you, Till heaven and earth pass, one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilled.* (Matthew 5:18) The words that outlast heaven and earth are tied to the law. Mark carries the saying word for word: *Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away.* (Mark 13:31)

The prophets already set the made things against the word. *The grass withereth, the flower fadeth: but the word of our Elohim (God) shall stand for ever.* (Isaiah 40:8) *Lift up your eyes to the heavens, and look upon the earth beneath: for the heavens shall vanish away like smoke, and the earth shall wax old like a garment, and they that dwell therein shall die in like manner: but my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness shall not be abolished.* (Isaiah 51:6) The psalm says the heavens are made: *Of old hast thou laid the foundation of the earth: and the heavens are the work of thy hands.* (Psalm 102:25) What he made passes, and the word he spoke does not.$n$,24,32,35,'free',377),
('matthew-24-the-day-and-hour-and-the-thief',$n$The day and hour, the taken and the left, and the thief$n$,$n$It ain't new. The hour is the Father's: *But of that day and hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels of heaven, but my Father only.* (Matthew 24:36) Mark names the Son in it: *But of that day and that hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels which are in heaven, neither the Son, but the Father.* (Mark 13:32) The Son submits to the Father's timing, and the disciples had asked it after the resurrection: *Adonai (Lord), wilt thou at this time restore again the kingdom to Yashar'el (Israel)?* (Acts 1:6) The psalm shows the same order: *Yahuah (LORD) said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool.* (Psalm 110:1)

Noah's day sets who is taken: *And knew not until the flood came, and took them all away; so shall also the coming of the Son of Adam be.* (Matthew 24:39) Then, *Then shall two be in the field; the one shall be taken, and the other left.* (Matthew 24:40) The harvest answers the order: *Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them: but gather the wheat into my barn.* (Matthew 13:30) The tares go first, and Luke has the same two in one bed: *the one shall be taken, and the other shall be left.* (Luke 17:34)

The watch is the household's: *But know this, that if the goodman of the house had known in what watch the thief would come, he would have watched, and would not have suffered his house to be broken up.* (Matthew 24:43) Paul says, *But of the times and the seasons, brethren, ye have no need that I write unto you.* (1 Thessalonians 5:1) Peter says, *But the day of Yahuah (Lord) will come as a thief in the night.* (2 Peter 3:10) And in Revelation, *Behold, I come as a thief. Blessed is he that watcheth, and keepeth his garments, lest he walk naked, and they see his shame.* (Revelation 16:15)$n$,24,36,44,'free',378);
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b4_a (slug TEXT, book TEXT, ch INT, a_vn INT, z_vn INT) ON COMMIT DROP;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s496_b4_m (thread_slug TEXT, card_id BIGINT, ord INT, member_note TEXT) ON COMMIT DROP;

CREATE OR REPLACE TEMP VIEW _s496_b4_lookup AS
SELECT b.slug AS book_slug, c.chapter_number, v.verse_number, v.id AS verse_id, v.text
  FROM verses v JOIN chapters c ON v.chapter_id = c.id JOIN books b ON c.book_id = b.id
  JOIN editions e ON b.edition_id = e.id
 WHERE e.slug = 'canon';

DO $s496$
DECLARE r RECORD; sid BIGINT; tid BIGINT; ltext TEXT; cur RECORD; n INT; av BIGINT; zv BIGINT;
        n_note INT := 0; n_note_same INT := 0; n_thr INT := 0; n_thr_same INT := 0; n_anc INT := 0; n_anc_same INT := 0; n_mem INT := 0; n_mem_same INT := 0; n_total INT;
BEGIN
  -- ---- guards: every row is checked before anything is written ----
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM _s496_b4_c LOOP
    SELECT verse_id, text INTO sid, ltext FROM _s496_b4_lookup WHERE book_slug = 'matthew' AND chapter_number = r.src_ch AND verse_number = r.src_v;
    IF sid IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: source verse matthew %:% missing', r.src_ch, r.src_v; END IF;
    IF ltext <> r.src_text THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: live text of matthew %:% differs from the read-back this migration was written against — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.src_ch, r.src_v; END IF;
    SELECT verse_id INTO tid FROM _s496_b4_lookup WHERE book_slug = r.tgt_slug AND chapter_number = r.tgt_ch AND verse_number = r.tgt_v;
    IF tid IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: target verse % %:% missing', r.tgt_slug, r.tgt_ch, r.tgt_v; END IF;
    SELECT * INTO cur FROM cross_references WHERE id = r.id;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: card % is gone', r.id; END IF;
    IF cur.source_verse_id <> sid OR cur.target_verse_id <> tid THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: card % no longer sits on matthew %:% -> % %:%', r.id, r.src_ch, r.src_v, r.tgt_slug, r.tgt_ch, r.tgt_v; END IF;
    IF cur.note IS DISTINCT FROM r.old_note AND cur.note IS DISTINCT FROM r.new_note THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: the live note of card % differs from the read-back this migration was written against — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.id; END IF;
    IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.id = mm.thread_id
                WHERE mm.cross_reference_id = r.id AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b4_m m WHERE m.card_id = r.id AND m.thread_slug = th.slug)) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: card % already sits in a thread this migration does not put it in — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.id; END IF;
  END LOOP;
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM _s496_b4_t LOOP
    IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b4_lookup WHERE book_slug = 'matthew' AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.a_vn)
    OR NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b4_lookup WHERE book_slug = 'matthew' AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.z_vn) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: anchor %:%-% for new thread % missing', r.ch, r.a_vn, r.z_vn, r.slug; END IF;
  END LOOP;
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM _s496_b4_a LOOP
    SELECT verse_id INTO av FROM _s496_b4_lookup WHERE book_slug = r.book AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.a_vn;
    SELECT verse_id INTO zv FROM _s496_b4_lookup WHERE book_slug = r.book AND chapter_number = r.ch AND verse_number = r.z_vn;
    IF av IS NULL OR zv IS NULL THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: anchor verse % %:%-% for thread % missing', r.book, r.ch, r.a_vn, r.z_vn, r.slug; END IF;
    SELECT * INTO cur FROM cross_reference_threads WHERE slug = r.slug;
    IF NOT FOUND THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: thread to anchor % does not exist', r.slug; END IF;
    IF cur.anchor_verse_id_start IS NOT NULL AND (cur.anchor_verse_id_start <> av OR cur.anchor_verse_id_end IS DISTINCT FROM zv) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: thread % already carries a different anchor — re-run the dump and rebuild', r.slug; END IF;
  END LOOP;
  FOR r IN SELECT DISTINCT thread_slug FROM _s496_b4_m LOOP
    IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_threads WHERE slug = r.thread_slug) AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM _s496_b4_t WHERE slug = r.thread_slug) THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: thread to join % does not exist', r.thread_slug; END IF;
  END LOOP;

  -- ---- notes rewritten in place ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b4_c;
  UPDATE cross_references x SET note = c.new_note FROM _s496_b4_c c WHERE x.id = c.id AND x.note IS DISTINCT FROM c.new_note;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_note = ROW_COUNT; n_note_same := n_total - n_note;

  -- ---- new threads ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b4_t;
  SELECT t.slug, t.title, t.summary_md, sv.verse_id, ev.verse_id, t.tier::content_tier, t.sort_order
    FROM _s496_b4_t t
    JOIN _s496_b4_lookup sv ON sv.book_slug='matthew' AND sv.chapter_number=t.ch AND sv.verse_number=t.a_vn
    JOIN _s496_b4_lookup ev ON ev.book_slug='matthew' AND ev.chapter_number=t.ch AND ev.verse_number=t.z_vn
  ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_thr = ROW_COUNT; n_thr_same := n_total - n_thr;

  -- ---- anchors for the unanchored threads these cards join ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b4_a;
  UPDATE cross_reference_threads th SET anchor_verse_id_start = sv.verse_id, anchor_verse_id_end = ev.verse_id
    FROM _s496_b4_a a
    JOIN _s496_b4_lookup sv ON sv.book_slug=a.book AND sv.chapter_number=a.ch AND sv.verse_number=a.a_vn
    JOIN _s496_b4_lookup ev ON ev.book_slug=a.book AND ev.chapter_number=a.ch AND ev.verse_number=a.z_vn
   WHERE th.slug = a.slug AND th.anchor_verse_id_start IS NULL;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_anc = ROW_COUNT; n_anc_same := n_total - n_anc;

  -- ---- members: each card into its thread, after the thread's live maximum, in Matthew verse order ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n_total FROM _s496_b4_m;
  SELECT th.id, m.card_id,
         COALESCE((SELECT max(mm.sort_order) FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm WHERE mm.thread_id = th.id), 0)
           + row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY th.id ORDER BY m.ord, m.card_id),
         m.member_note
    FROM _s496_b4_m m JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.slug = m.thread_slug
   WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm WHERE mm.thread_id = th.id AND mm.cross_reference_id = m.card_id)
  ON CONFLICT (thread_id, cross_reference_id) DO NOTHING;
  GET DIAGNOSTICS n_mem = ROW_COUNT; n_mem_same := n_total - n_mem;

  -- ---- proof before commit: every card carries its new note and sits in its thread ----
  SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM _s496_b4_c c JOIN cross_references x ON x.id = c.id WHERE x.note IS DISTINCT FROM c.new_note;
  IF n > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: % card(s) do not carry the new note', n; END IF;
  SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM _s496_b4_m m WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM cross_reference_thread_members mm JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.id = mm.thread_id AND th.slug = m.thread_slug WHERE mm.cross_reference_id = m.card_id);
  IF n > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: % member row(s) did not land', n; END IF;
  SELECT count(*) INTO n FROM _s496_b4_a a JOIN cross_reference_threads th ON th.slug = a.slug WHERE th.anchor_verse_id_start IS NULL;
  IF n > 0 THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'S496: % thread(s) still unanchored', n; END IF;

  RAISE NOTICE 'S496 (matthew b4) summary: notes rewritten % / already there %; threads inserted % / already there %; anchors set % / already there %; members inserted % / already there %',
    n_note, n_note_same, n_thr, n_thr_same, n_anc, n_anc_same, n_mem, n_mem_same;
END $s496$;
COMMIT;
\echo 'session496 — Matthew 20–24 rebuild done.'
